---
import DocPage from '../../components/DocPage.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
const qa = [
  'git clone https://github.com/gua-project/gua.git',
  'cd gua',
  'cmake --preset windows-qa -DGUA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF',
  'cmake --build --preset windows-qa --target gua gua-runtime gua-godot',
  'pwsh -NoProfile -File scripts/stage-remote-build.ps1 -BuildDirectory build/windows-qa -OutputDirectory artifacts/qa -Flavor qa -Remote on -GodotExtension build/windows-qa/stage/addons/gua/bin/gua_godot.windows.release.x86_64.dll',
].join('\n');
const dev = [
  'cmake -S . -B build/dev-remote -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64 -DGUA_DISTRIBUTION_FLAVOR=development -DGUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION=ON -DGUA_STAGE_IN_SAMPLE=OFF -DGUA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DGUA_GODOT_CONFIGURATION=Debug',
  'cmake --build build/dev-remote --config Debug --target gua gua-runtime gua-godot',
].join('\n');
const godot = [
  'extends Control',
  'const Adapter = preload("res://addons/gua/gua_auto_adapter.gd")',
  'var ui = Adapter.new()',
  'func _ready() -> void:',
  '  ui.attach(self)',
  '  ui.update("title")',
  '  if ui.get_build_info().get("remoteEnabled", false):',
  '    if not ui.start_inspector_bridge(8765):',
  '      push_error("The compiled bridge could not start")',
  'func _process(_delta: float) -> void:',
  '  ui.update("title")',
].join('\n');
---
<DocPage eyebrow="BUILD / REMOTE AUTOMATION" title="Choose a build that can be controlled remotely" lead="For the upcoming v1.2.0, choose the native capability before compiling and packaging. A session profile can narrow that capability; it cannot add missing code." description="Gua v1.2.0 upcoming remote build opt-in, Shipping, QA, Development, AI Player, Godot, Unity, native manifests, and migration from v1.1.2." section="remote-builds">
  <Callout kind="warn" title="v1.2.0 is upcoming, not a published package"><p>This page describes the next version's build contract already present in the Gua source. It does not announce a v1.2.0 tag, release asset, or registry package. For current v1.1.2 installations, keep the game add-on, native libraries, managed assemblies, and test packages at matching versions. Do not apply the new API examples to an old native DLL.</p><p><a href="/docs/release-1-2-0/">v1.2.0 migration note</a> · <a href="/godot/ui/">Godot installation</a> · <a href="/unity/ui/">Unity installation</a></p></Callout>
  <h2 id="three-choices">Three separate choices</h2>
  <ol><li><strong>Debug or Release</strong> controls the build configuration. Release is not proof that remote access is absent, and Debug alone does not enable it.</li><li><strong>Compiled capability</strong> is chosen with <code>GUA_DISTRIBUTION_FLAVOR</code> and <code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION</code> when building native Gua. It decides whether remote transports and dispatch exist in the artifact.</li><li><strong>Debug or Player profile</strong> controls the view and permitted operations within the compiled ceiling. <code>GUA_OBSERVATION_PROFILE=player</code> restricts an available runtime; it does not create a connection or compile in a bridge.</li></ol>
  <h2 id="matrix">Choose the artifact for your purpose</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose / flavor</th><th>Native remote flag</th><th>Configuration</th><th>Remote ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Normal distribution: <code>shipping</code></td><td><code>OFF</code> required; default</td><td>Release</td><td>None: remote implementation excluded</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Development / Editor: <code>development</code></td><td><code>OFF</code> for local use; explicit <code>ON</code> for remote use</td><td>Debug or Release</td><td>Debug when ON; host can narrow to Player</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Release test executable: <code>qa</code></td><td>Explicit <code>ON</code> required</td><td>Release</td><td>Debug; host can narrow to Player</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Released game played by an AI: <code>ai-player</code></td><td>Explicit <code>ON</code> required</td><td>Release</td><td>Player, fixed at compile time</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>A fresh native build defaults to <code>shipping</code> and <code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION=OFF</code>. Shipping+ON, QA/AI-player+OFF, and a non-Release distribution build are rejected. The local core, observation/recording APIs, and compatible bridge function signatures are retained where needed. A Shipping bridge call is a failure stub: the exported function name is not evidence of a usable remote implementation.</p>
  <Callout title="Two release executables with different purposes"><p>Use <code>qa</code> for a tester that needs Debug inspection of a Release executable. Use <code>ai-player</code> when the shipped game deliberately supports an AI participant. For a game that should not be controlled through Gua, distribute <code>shipping</code>. Do not ship all variants and choose one with an environment variable.</p></Callout>
  <h2 id="build">Build from the matching source</h2>
  <p>Run these commands from the Gua source checkout containing this contract. Until v1.2.0 is released, use the appropriate source revision for evaluation rather than assuming a <code>gua-v1.2.0</code> tag or package exists. Use Git, CMake and a C++20 toolchain. The Windows presets below require Visual Studio 18 2026 C++ tools and a CMake version supporting that generator. Godot native builds also need Python and godot-cpp dependencies; the first configure may fetch those dependencies. Staging/probing scripts require PowerShell 7 and .NET 10 SDK.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Windows preset</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>windows-shipping</code></td><td>Shipping / OFF / Release</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>windows-qa</code></td><td>QA / ON / Release</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>windows-ai-player</code></td><td>AI Player / ON / Release</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>windows-msvc-debug</code>, <code>windows-msvc-release</code></td><td>Existing development fixtures: explicitly ON, not normal Shipping</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <h3>A QA Release executable with a Godot add-on</h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Windows / QA / Release — source build and isolated package" lang="powershell" code={qa} />
  <p>The last command probes the actual native libraries, checks their identity, prepares the add-on, and verifies the package. The destination must be empty. Copy <code>artifacts/qa/addons/gua</code> into the game's export staging copy and export using the Release extension. This Release-only add-on does not supply the Editor/Debug extension; keep the development Editor setup separate. Do not copy only a DLL into an old add-on containing other variants.</p>
  <p>For normal Shipping, use <code>windows-shipping</code>, <code>build/windows-shipping</code>, <code>artifacts/shipping</code>, <code>-Flavor shipping -Remote off</code>, and the corresponding Release extension path. For AI Player, use <code>windows-ai-player</code>, <code>build/windows-ai-player</code>, <code>artifacts/ai-player</code>, and <code>-Flavor ai-player -Remote on</code>. Keep separate build and staging directories.</p>
  <h3>Development / Editor with an explicit opt-in</h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Isolated Debug development build" lang="powershell" code={dev} />
  <p>For a local-only development build, configure a fresh <code>build/dev-local</code> with <code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION=OFF</code>. For a Release development build, keep the flavor <code>development</code> and select Release for both native and Godot configuration. When staging Debug, pass <code>-Configuration Debug -EngineBuildKind debug</code> and the Debug extension path. <code>GUA_STAGE_IN_SAMPLE</code> defaults OFF and may be ON only for development; the two legacy development presets deliberately turn it ON.</p>
  <p>For a C++/.NET-only source build, configure with <code>-DGUA_BUILD_GODOT=OFF</code>, build <code>gua</code> and <code>gua-runtime</code>, and omit <code>-GodotExtension</code> from staging. On a single-configuration generator such as Ninja, set <code>-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release</code> for Shipping, QA, or AI Player in addition to the same flavor/remote flags. <code>GUA_BUILD_WS_BRIDGE=ON</code> alone is not an opt-in and is rejected when the main remote flag is OFF.</p>
  <h2 id="godot">Godot: select the add-on before exporting</h2>
  <p>Use the staged add-on for the selected flavor and native configuration. Godot's Debug/Release feature selection and <code>OS.has_feature()</code> choose or inspect resources; they do not remove remote code from a prebuilt GDExtension. The desktop staging tool emits the selected library descriptor and excludes the Shipping WebMCP resource and attachment code.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="v1.2.0 API: keep local collection when remote is absent" lang="gdscript" code={godot} />
  <p>On desktop, <code>remoteEnabled=true</code> permits attempting the bridge; a failed start can still mean a port conflict or startup error. On Godot Web, use the supported same-page WebMCP path rather than a native WebSocket listener. The profile still cannot exceed the native ceiling.</p>
  <h2 id="unity">Unity: native and managed compilation must agree</h2>
  <ol><li>Build or obtain a native core/runtime pair for the chosen flavor. Replacing only a game-side <code>#if</code> does not change a precompiled native DLL or static archive.</li><li>When compiling the Unity adapter source, define <code>GUA_REMOTE_AUTOMATION</code> for a remote-enabled adapter. Define <code>GUA_PLAYER_ONLY</code> as well for AI Player; it requires the first define. For Shipping, compile without these defines. Project defines do not rebuild a precompiled <code>Gua.Unity.dll</code>: select or rebuild the matching assembly/package.</li><li>Set <code>GUA_BUILD_VARIANT</code> in the Player build process to <code>shipping</code>, <code>development</code>, <code>qa</code>, or <code>ai-player</code>. Its default is <code>shipping</code>. It tells the prebuild guard what to verify; it does not modify native capabilities. For example, <code>$env:GUA_BUILD_VARIANT = "ai-player"</code> is only the build-intent setting after preparing the AI Player native and managed artifacts.</li><li>QA, Shipping, and AI Player require a non-Development Player build. The guard checks compiled managed capability, selected native identity, and manifest/hash agreement before generation. Resolve a mismatch by replacing the incorrect artifact, not by renaming it.</li></ol>
  <p>The repository sample intentionally opts into development automation. Its <code>GuaUnityEditorCommands.BuildPlayer</code> helper takes intent from <code>-guaVariant</code>, defaulting to <code>development</code>, and sets <code>GUA_BUILD_VARIANT</code> itself. When using that helper, pass the intended option such as <code>-guaVariant ai-player</code>; setting the environment variable alone is overwritten by the helper's default. This is not a normal Shipping build command, and the option does not rebuild native libraries or change managed defines. Supply the matching artifacts first.</p>
  <h2 id="packages">Packages and manifests</h2>
  <p>A download is not made Shipping merely because its file name contains “Release.” The existing development presets and SDK build workflows explicitly opt in. Select a verified artifact with the intended flavor; do not assume that every future variant already has a published download.</p>
  <ul><li>Use separate artifact/staging roots for Shipping, QA, Development, and AI Player. Keep the native core/runtime pair, Godot extension, Unity managed assembly, and Web resources consistent.</li><li>Preserve <code>gua-build-manifest.json</code> and its native identity/hash checks. Unknown, extra, stale, or mismatched native files must fail packaging/build validation. Renaming a QA DLL does not make it safe to distribute.</li><li>Native NuGet packing requires <code>GuaNativeAssetsRoot</code> with verified manifests and binaries for <code>win-x64</code>, <code>linux-x64</code>, <code>osx-x64</code>, and <code>osx-arm64</code>. The pack properties default to <code>GuaDistributionFlavor=shipping</code> and <code>GuaEnableRemoteAutomation=false</code>; QA packing must explicitly use <code>qa</code> and <code>true</code>. These properties validate existing binaries; they do not recompile them. The old unlabeled single-DLL fallback is refused.</li><li>Native NuGet packages carry component manifests such as <code>gua-core-build-manifest.json</code> and <code>gua-runtime-build-manifest.json</code>. Keep versions and build identity aligned across dependencies. Do not publish different variants under the same package ID/version to the same feed.</li></ul>
  <h3 id="web">Web compilation is also explicit</h3>
  <p>Activate the matching Emscripten toolchain first: <code>emcmake</code> and <code>node</code> must be on PATH. The source helper <code>scripts/build-web-native.ps1</code> accepts <code>-Flavor</code> and <code>-Remote</code>; its legacy defaults are <code>development</code> and <code>on</code>, with Debug configuration. Distribution flavors require <code>-Configuration Release</code>. For example, <code>pwsh -File scripts/build-web-native.ps1 -RuntimeOnly -Configuration Release -Flavor ai-player -Remote on -BuildDirectory build/web-ai-player</code> builds Unity static archives and checks their linked identity under Node. Use a separate directory and <code>-Flavor shipping -Remote off</code> for local-only Shipping compilation.</p>
  <p>This helper's native/WASM outputs are not by themselves a verified final Godot Web or Unity WebGL package. Complete the engine-specific resource/assembly selection and export validation too. Shipping must omit the WebMCP implementation and entry points; a runtime check around attachment is not code removal. The desktop <code>stage-remote-build.ps1</code> probe is not a WASM package verifier. See <a href="/webmcp/">WebMCP</a> for same-page operation and supported browser features.</p>
  <h2 id="inspect">Check the compiled capability first</h2>
  <p>Read immutable build-info using <code>gua_copy_build_info_json</code> / <code>gua_runtime_copy_build_info_json</code> in C/C++, <code>Gua.Core.GuaBuildInfo.GetJson()</code> / <code>GuaRuntime.GetBuildInfoJson()</code> in .NET, or <code>ui.get_build_info()</code> / native context <code>get_build_info_json()</code> in Godot. Check <code>flavor</code>, <code>configuration</code>, <code>remoteEnabled</code>, <code>remotePermissionCeiling</code>, <code>buildId</code>, <code>sourceCommit</code>, and <code>transports</code>.</p>
  <p>Shipping cannot recover the absent implementation through environment variables, startup arguments, or bridge APIs. AI Player starts in Player and rejects Debug elevation through APIs, reset/reconnect, client factories, and borrowed/unscoped consumer paths. Existing <a href="/agent-policy/">Player exposure rules</a> and separate <a href="/game-input/">game-input permissions</a> still apply at enqueue and host consumption. A Player build does not automatically grant keyboard, pointer, or gameplay-action access.</p>
  <p>If <code>remoteEnabled=false</code>, inability to connect is expected; changing the port or setting the Player profile cannot enable it. If it is true, check startup, profile/transport support, and port use. A missing listener alone does not prove that code was removed: keep package identity, compile/link inspection, and positive/negative tests in the distribution pipeline. For example, the Gua source provides <code>verify-remote-build.ps1</code> and <code>verify-remote-build-graph.ps1</code> (Windows/MSVC).</p>
  <h2 id="migrate">Migrate an existing v1.1.2 project</h2>
  <ol class="steps"><li><strong>Choose the intended use.</strong><p>Normal shipped game → Shipping; automated Debug inspection of a Release executable → QA; opt-in Editor testing → Development; AI participant in a shipped game → AI Player.</p></li><li><strong>Replace the complete integration.</strong><p>Use matching-version native, managed, add-on, and test components. Remove stale copies from old bin/plugin directories; do not keep the old QA DLL as a fallback.</p></li><li><strong>Configure a fresh build directory.</strong><p>Set the flavor and explicit remote flag together. A cached old WS option or a Release export preset is not the new opt-in.</p></li><li><strong>Recompile the layers you own.</strong><p>Build native Gua with the selected capability. For Unity source adapters, match the managed defines; for precompiled adapters, obtain the matching build. Keep Player policy as a separate host decision.</p></li><li><strong>Stage, verify, then export.</strong><p>Use an empty destination, preserve manifests, and reject mismatches. Verify that normal Shipping cannot start a bridge, AI Player cannot gain Debug and can perform only allowed operations, and QA/Development remote operations still work when enabled.</p></li><li><strong>Update CI and test expectations.</strong><p>Install the selected variant rather than blindly taking a development SDK. A remote test cannot operate a normal Shipping artifact; test that absence separately and run interactive scenarios against QA or permitted AI Player artifacts.</p></li></ol>
  <p>Authoritative source: <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/cmake/GuaRemoteBuild.cmake">native flags and validation</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/CMakePresets.json">presets</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/protocol/specs/remote-build-variants.md">build contract</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/scripts/stage-remote-build.ps1">desktop staging</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/bindings/unity/Editor/GuaVariantBuildGuard.cs">Unity build guard</a></p>
</DocPage>
